  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Chef Barinov announces to the entire restaurant that he is retiring from the stove and handing the title of head chef to his best friend and sous-chef, Lyova. Yet Lyova declines the promotion of a lifetime to fly to Bishkek after Gulya.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Лёва, хорошо, что ты встал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я должен сделать важное заявление.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я больше не работаю шефом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я за ресторан не переживаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Лёва большой профессионал и друг.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он мой самый лучший друг.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Пришла пора вам стать шефом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "lyova",
      "t": "Шеф, простите, я не могу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "lyova",
      "t": "Я сейчас улетаю в Бишкек.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "lyova",
      "t": "Улетаю, может, на пять лет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "lyova",
      "t": "Извините меня за это решение.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Виктор Петрович, это настоящая любовь.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Лёва, хорошо, что ты встал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Lyova, it is good that you stood up.' Chef Barinov anchors the line on 'Lyova', while 'stood up' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я должен сделать важное заявление.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I must make an important announcement.' In Russian, the idea of 'I' flows naturally into 'statement' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я больше не работаю шефом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am no longer working as head chef.' The negative particle sits right before the verb meaning 'work' to negate the statement.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я за ресторан не переживаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am not worried about the restaurant.' Chef Barinov brings out the contrast between 'I' and 'for' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Лёва большой профессионал и друг.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Lyova is a great professional and friend.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'Lyova' before landing on 'friend'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он мой самый лучший друг.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He is my very best friend.' Chef Barinov brings out the contrast between 'he' and 'my' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Пришла пора вам стать шефом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The time has come for you to become head chef.' Chef Barinov anchors the line on 'came', while 'to become' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lyova",
      "t": "Шеф, простите, я не могу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Chef, forgive me, I cannot.' The grammar packs 'Chef Barinov' and 'can' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lyova",
      "t": "Я сейчас улетаю в Бишкек.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am flying to Bishkek right now.' The meaning hinges on 'I' working together with 'Bishkek' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lyova",
      "t": "Улетаю, может, на пять лет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am flying away, perhaps for five years.' In Russian, the idea of 'am flying away' flows naturally into 'years' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lyova",
      "t": "Извините меня за это решение.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Excuse me for this decision.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'excuse me' and finishing on 'decision'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Виктор Петрович, это настоящая любовь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Viktor Petrovich, this is true love.' Notice how Russian expresses 'this' and 'love' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
